= Energy eigenvalue

An energy eigenvalue is an <eigenvalue> of the <Hamiltonian operator> $H$: there is a nonzero <vector> $\psi$ in its <operator domain> $D(H)$ with
$$
H\psi=E\psi.
$$
For a <self-adjoint operator>, $E$ is real. Normalizing $\psi$ produces an <energy eigenstate>; an ideal <energy measurement> then returns $E$ with probability one. The associated <energy eigenspace> includes all vectors with this same <energy eigenvalue>, including the zero vector, which represents no <quantum state>. Different <energy eigenvalues> have orthogonal <energy eigenspaces>. https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf[Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, Lemma 2.20]{ref}.

The <energy eigenvalues> form the <point spectrum>, which can be only part of the <spectrum>. The term <discrete spectrum> more specifically means isolated <eigenvalues> of finite multiplicity. The usual fixed-nucleus <hydrogen atom> model has discrete negative <bound-state> <energy eigenvalues> together with a continuum at nonnegative <energy>. https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf[Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, sections 6.4 and 10.2]{ref}. A <quantum system> need not have a ladder of discrete <energy eigenvalues>: a whole-line <free particle> has <continuous spectrum>. A <plane wave> used for a <free particle> is a generalized <eigenfunction> of the <Hamiltonian operator>, rather than a normalized vector in the physical <Hilbert space>. https://live.ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/61bc31b8d8bf0680c322733910a71aa0_MIT8_05F13_Chap_01.pdf[Barton Zwiebach, MIT 8.05 Wave Mechanics, sections 2 and 4]{ref}.

For the <infinite square well> $0<x<L$, the <Dirichlet boundary conditions> give
$$
\phi_n(x)=\sqrt{\frac2L}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L},\qquad E_n=\frac{\hbar^2\pi^2 n^2}{2mL^2},\qquad n=1,2,\ldots.
$$
Substitution into $H\phi_n=E_n\phi_n$ and <wavefunction normalization> verify these statements directly. Here $E_1$ is the <ground-state energy>, even though its index is one; the labeling convention does not determine which <quantum state> is lowest. Equal populations of $\phi_1$ and $\phi_2$ have <energy> <expectation value> $(E_1+E_2)/2=5E_1/2$, but an <energy measurement> returns $E_1$ or $4E_1$, each with probability $1/2$. An average of allowed <energy eigenvalues> need not itself be an <energy eigenvalue>.

= Energy eigenstate
{parent=Energy eigenvalue}
{title2=$H\phi=E\phi$}

An <energy eigenstate> is a <quantum state> represented by an <eigenvector> of the <quantum Hamiltonian>. For a time-independent <quantum Hamiltonian>, a normalized <eigenfunction> $\phi$ with <energy eigenvalue> $E$ evolves according to the <Schrödinger equation> as
$$
\psi(x,t)=\phi(x)e^{-iEt/\hbar},\qquad |\psi(x,t)|^2=|\phi(x)|^2.
$$
Here $\hbar=h/(2\pi)$ is the reduced <Planck constant>. The changing factor is a <global phase>, so this is a <stationary state>: its position <probability density> is constant. For example, the second <infinite square well> mode has two density lobes separated by a <node of a standing wave> at $x=L/2$; both lobes stay fixed while its <quantum phase> rotates. An ideal <energy measurement> returns $E$ with probability one, but a <position measurement> still has a distribution. Stationary density does not describe a particle sitting at the peak of a lobe. Distinct <energy eigenvalues> accumulate different <quantum phases>, making their <quantum superposition> behave differently.

= Energy eigenfunction
{parent=Energy eigenstate}
{title2=$\hat H\psi=E\psi$}

A nonzero position-space <wavefunction> representing an <energy eigenstate>. For a time-independent <quantum Hamiltonian>, its time evolution is a <global phase>, so its position <probability density> is stationary.

= Energy eigenvector
{parent=Energy eigenstate}

A nonzero <eigenvector> of the <quantum Hamiltonian>.

= Energy eigenspace
{parent=Energy eigenvalue}

The energy eigenspace at $E$ is the <vector subspace>
$$
\mathcal E_E=\ker(H-EI)=\{\psi\in D(H):H\psi=E\psi\},
$$
where $H$ is the <Hamiltonian operator> and $I$ is the <identity operator>. It is nonzero exactly when $E$ is an <energy eigenvalue>. Its <dimension> is the multiplicity of that <energy eigenvalue>; <degenerate energy levels> have multiplicity greater than one. The <ground-state subspace> is $\mathcal E_{E_0}$, and its <dimension> is the <ground-state degeneracy>. A vector in an <energy eigenspace> need not be one of a specially chosen <orthonormal basis> of <energy eigenstates>.

<Linearity> makes the useful point immediate: if $H\phi_1=E\phi_1$ and $H\phi_2=E\phi_2$, then $H(a\phi_1+b\phi_2)=E(a\phi_1+b\phi_2)$. Every normalized nonzero combination stays in the same <energy eigenspace>. For a time-independent <Hamiltonian operator>, its evolution is multiplication by the common <global phase> $e^{-iEt/\hbar}$, so it is a <stationary state>. A <quantum superposition> within a degenerate <energy eigenspace> can change spatial shape when its coefficients are chosen differently, but its density does not subsequently beat under this unchanged <Hamiltonian operator>.

Across distinct <energy eigenspaces>, relative <quantum phases> evolve. For orthonormal <eigenfunctions> $\phi_1,\phi_2$ of the <Hamiltonian operator> with <energy eigenvalues> $E_1\ne E_2$, an equal-weight <quantum superposition> has
$$
\psi(x,t)=\frac{e^{-iE_1t/\hbar}\phi_1(x)+e^{-iE_2t/\hbar}\phi_2(x)}{\sqrt2},
$$
and position <probability density>
$$
|\psi(x,t)|^2=\frac{|\phi_1(x)|^2+|\phi_2(x)|^2}{2}+\operatorname{Re}\!\left[\phi_1(x)^*\phi_2(x)e^{-i(E_2-E_1)t/\hbar}\right].
$$
The interference term can change local probabilities even though the <Born rule> assigns the same time-independent probability $1/2$ to each <energy measurement> outcome. <Orthogonality> makes its integral zero, preserving <wavefunction normalization>. This <pure state> is not a <stationary state>; some particular measurement distributions may nevertheless remain constant. An incoherent <mixed state> with the same energy populations has no such interference term.

\Image[ground-excited-states-stationary-density.png]
{title=An excited energy eigenstate can be stationary while a two-energy superposition beats}
{description=Exact <infinite square well> densities, not measured data, with $u=x/L$ and $\theta=(E_2-E_1)t/\hbar$. The left panel shows the unchanged density of the second <energy eigenstate> at three times. The right panel shows a coherent equal-weight <quantum superposition> at $\theta=0,\pi/2,\pi$; its energy populations remain $1/2,1/2$. The dashed curve is the density of the corresponding incoherent <mixed state>.}

For the pictured <infinite square well>, $\theta=3E_1t/\hbar$ and the interference repeats after $T=2\pi\hbar/(3E_1)$. At $t=0$, direct integration gives $\langle x\rangle=L/2-16L/(9\pi^2)$; halfway through the period it is $L/2+16L/(9\pi^2)$. This is a moving <expectation value>, rather than a trajectory of an individual particle. The distinction between an <energy eigenspace> and a combination of different <energy eigenspaces> is what makes the different behavior possible.

= Excited state
{parent=Energy eigenvalue}
{wiki}

In the stationary-state terminology used here, an excited state is a normalized <energy eigenstate> with <energy eigenvalue> $E>E_0$, where $E_0$ is an attained <ground-state energy>. Its excitation cost above the <ground state> is $\Delta E=E-E_0>0$. Several <energy eigenstates> can share one excited <energy eigenvalue> through <degenerate energy levels>. An arbitrary <quantum superposition> with <energy> <expectation value> above $E_0$ need not be a single stationary <excited state>; its energy content is described by its <Born rule> probabilities or, for a continuum, its <scalar spectral measure>.

An <excited state> can be a <stationary state>: the second <infinite square well> mode is an immediate example. Spontaneous <photon emission> is a transition caused by coupling matter to the <electromagnetic field>; it is absent from an isolated matter-only <Hamiltonian operator>. The relevant <energy eigenstate> of that isolated model is therefore not automatically an <energy eigenstate> of the full interacting system. This distinction explains how an ideal stationary <excited state> can approximate a state with a finite experimental lifetime. See <electric-dipole interaction> for the coupling used in the simplest optical model.

For a one-photon transition between stationary internal states, neglecting recoil, <conservation of energy> requires
$$
h\nu=E_f-E_i\quad\text{for absorption},\qquad h\nu=E_i-E_f\quad\text{for emission}.
$$
Here $h$ is the <Planck constant> and $\nu$ is the <photon> <frequency>. Energy matching alone does not guarantee a transition: in the <electric-dipole interaction> approximation its strength also involves the <matrix element> $\langle f|\mathbf d\cdot\boldsymbol\epsilon|i\rangle$, where $\mathbf d$ is the <electric dipole moment> operator and $\boldsymbol\epsilon$ specifies <photon polarization>. A zero <matrix element> gives an <electric-dipole selection rule>. Thus an absent optical line does not prove the corresponding <energy eigenvalue> is absent; a different interaction can couple different states. https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/qmech/lectures/node121.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Quantum Mechanics, Selection Rules]{ref}. The <Franck-Hertz experiment> gives a particularly instructive comparison between collision excitation and <photon emission>.

The <first excited state> terminology assumes a least <energy eigenvalue> above $E_0$. Such a next level is not guaranteed by <quantum mechanics>: a <continuous spectrum> can start immediately above the spectral bottom, or <energy eigenvalues> can accumulate there. For example, a <Hamiltonian operator> with <energy eigenvalues> $0,\varepsilon,\varepsilon/2,\varepsilon/3,\ldots$, where $\varepsilon>0$, has a <ground-state energy> zero but no least positive <energy eigenvalue>: every candidate $\varepsilon/n$ is exceeded downward by $\varepsilon/(n+1)$. A <free particle> on the whole line has neither a <ground state> nor a first stationary <excited state>. Each use of a discrete excitation ladder therefore needs a specified <Hamiltonian operator> and its <boundary conditions>.

= Intersystem crossing
{parent=Excited state}

A nonradiative transition between electronic states of different <spin multiplicity>, enabled by interactions such as spin–orbit coupling. A constant intersystem-crossing channel contributes to the unquenched excited-state lifetime.

= Franck-Hertz experiment
{c}
{parent=Excited state}
{wiki=Franck–Hertz_experiment}

The Franck-Hertz experiment connects a reproducible loss of <electron> <kinetic energy> with excitation of a <chemical atom>. The original mercury-vapor experiments were performed during 1912–1914 while investigating <ionization>; they were not designed as a test of the <Bohr model>. Their later interpretation distinguished internal excitation from removal of an <electron>. https://labs.physik.kit.edu/prakt-klass-physik-manuals/P2/Franck_Hertz_Versuch/[KIT physics laboratory, H23 Franck-Hertz-Versuch, Motivation]{ref}.

In a modern mercury tube, a heated <cathode> emits <electrons> and a positive perforated grid accelerates them through Hg vapor. A collector slightly below the grid's <electric potential> rejects slow <electrons>. The oven controls vapor density and hence the <mean free path>. Keep heating and retarding settings fixed, sweep the accelerating <electric potential difference> $V$, and record collector <electric current> $I(V)$. These measured quantities indirectly probe atomic excitation. https://physlab.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/03/Franck_exp_v12015.pdf[Shah, Alam and Anwar, Electron energy loss investigated through the Franck-Hertz Experiment, sections 3–4]{ref}.

The curve shows repeated current minima, typically about $4.9\,\mathrm V$ apart. In the collision model, an <electron> gains approximately $eV$ of <kinetic energy>, with $e$ the positive <elementary charge>. An <inelastic collision> transfers about $4.9\,\mathrm{eV}$ to internal excitation. Near the grid, this can prevent the <electron> crossing the retarding region, lowering collected <electric current>. Further acceleration restores collection; repeated excitation losses produce further minima. These represent multiple collisions, rather than equally spaced atomic <energy eigenvalues>. Contact potentials shift the first minimum, so compare several successive spacings instead of identifying the first dip's voltage with the excitation cost. https://physlab.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/03/Franck_exp_v12015.pdf[Shah, Alam and Anwar, sections 3.1–4 and Figure 3]{ref}.

The optical comparison supplies a second observable: mercury radiation at a <wavelength> near $253.6\,\mathrm{nm}$ was detected through a window in the tube. Its inferred <photon> <energy> $hc/\lambda\approx4.89\,\mathrm{eV}$ agrees with the characteristic collision loss, with $c$ the <speed of light>. This agreement supports the interpretation as a transition between internal atomic energies. It does not make the collector trace a complete spectrum. In particular, the lowest excited mercury level, labeled $6\,{}^3P_0$, is metastable because an <electric-dipole selection rule> forbids its direct transition to the <ground state>; the nearby $6\,{}^3P_1$ level emits the quoted <ultraviolet> line. Collision excitation and optical detection therefore have different sensitivities to the available <excited states>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-13-14-experimental-physics-i-ii-junior-lab-fall-2016-spring-2017/afdfff9f8bbe067239af19c8b178a764_MIT8_13-14F16-S17exp7.pdf[MIT Junior Lab, The Franck-Hertz Experiment, section VI and the discussion following Figure 6]{ref}.

Initial <electron> <kinetic energy> spread and varying collision positions broaden real minima. Repeat traces at different retarding settings and oven temperatures to distinguish excitation from collection effects. This probes a discrete energy-transfer channel, rather than every <energy eigenspace>, its <dimension>, or an absolute <ground-state energy>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-13-14-experimental-physics-i-ii-junior-lab-fall-2016-spring-2017/afdfff9f8bbe067239af19c8b178a764_MIT8_13-14F16-S17exp7.pdf[MIT Junior Lab, sections IV and VI]{ref}.

<James Franck> and <Gustav Ludwig Hertz> presented their paper on 24 April 1914. Its heated <platinum> wire sat inside a cylindrical <platinum> mesh, with a collector 1–2 mm beyond it. A heated bath and pumping prepared the vapor; guard rings suppressed leakage. They differentiated retarding-<electric current> scans to study outgoing <energies>.<Über Zusammenstöße zwischen Elektronen und den Molekülen des Quecksilberdampfes und die Ionisierungsspannung desselben by James Franck and Gustav Ludwig Hertz>[1914, pp. 457, 459–461, Figs. 1–2]{ref}

Their 1925 Nobel Prize preceded their lectures of 11 December 1926. <James Franck> described choosing <pressure> for many <collisions> before the mesh but few afterward.<Nobel Lectures, Physics 1922–1941/Transformations of Kinetic Energy of Free Electrons into Excitation Energy of Atoms by Impacts by James Franck>[Nobel Lectures, Physics 1922–1941; pp. 98, 100–101]{ref} <Gustav Ludwig Hertz> explained how a field-free collision region narrowed excitation thresholds.<Nobel Lectures, Physics 1922–1941/The Results of the Electron-Impact Tests in the Light of Bohr’s Theory of Atoms by Gustav Ludwig Hertz>[Nobel Lectures, Physics 1922–1941; pp. 112, 119–120]{ref}

= Franck-Hertz energy-loss model
{c}
{parent=Franck-Hertz experiment}
{title2=$\Delta,\ d=\Delta/e$}

A useful <Franck-Hertz energy-loss model> separates three operations: accelerating an <electron>, losing a discrete <energy> $\Delta$ in an <inelastic collision>, and selecting the surviving <electrons> with a retarding collector. The collector measures an accepted fraction of an outgoing <energy> distribution. This makes the experiment a small <inverse problem>: the <electric current> depends on both the collision physics and the readout.

For a deliberately simple one-channel model, let $u\geq0$ be the incoming <kinetic energy>, and let $p(u)$ be the <probability> that one encounter excites the <chemical atom>. Set $p(u)=0$ for $u<\Delta$ and $0\leq p(u)\leq1$ otherwise. The conditional <probability distribution> of outgoing <kinetic energy> $E$ is the discrete loss kernel
$$
K(E\mid u)=[1-p(u)]\delta(E-u)+p(u)\delta(E-u+\Delta).
$$
Here $\delta$ is the <Dirac delta function>. The kernel has unit <integral>, no negative outgoing <energy>, and mean outgoing <kinetic energy> $u-p(u)\Delta$. It neglects the small recoil loss in an <elastic collision>, other excitation channels and <ionization>. The threshold permits excitation; it does not require $p(u)=1$ above threshold. In particular, choosing a single $\Delta=4.9\,\mathrm{eV}$ here isolates the characteristic channel discussed above without claiming it is mercury's lowest possible excitation.

= Franck-Hertz retarding collector transfer
{c}
{parent=Franck-Hertz energy-loss model}
{title2=$T(E)=H(E-eV_r)$}

For <electrons> directed normally toward an ideal planar collector, a retarding <electric potential difference> $V_r>0$ imposes the barrier $eV_r$. Write its transfer as $T(E)=H(E-eV_r)$, where $H$ is the <Heaviside step function> and $e$ the positive <elementary charge>. Thus a perfectly sharp loss need not produce a sharp drop in <electric current>: the drop also depends on where the outgoing <energy> lies relative to the barrier. For an oblique incident <electron> at angle $\theta$ to the normal, the same planar model instead tests $E\cos^2\theta>eV_r$; angular scattering can change collection even at unchanged total <kinetic energy>.

\Image[franck-hertz-collector-threshold.png]
{title=<Franck-Hertz energy-loss model>: separate acceleration, loss and collection}
{description=Original functional schematic, not a scale reconstruction of a historical tube. The lower panel applies an ideal normally incident retarder to the explicitly chosen one-collision example. Equal vertical strokes mark outgoing <energies>; their labeled weights give <probabilities>.}
{source=franck-hertz-collector-threshold.py}

For example, choose a monoenergetic incoming beam with $u=5.6\,\mathrm{eV}$, $p=0.75$ and $\Delta=4.9\,\mathrm{eV}$. After one encounter, $25\%$ have $5.6\,\mathrm{eV}$ and $75\%$ have $0.7\,\mathrm{eV}$. A $1.0\,\mathrm V$ retarder collects only $0.25I_0$, whereas a $0.5\,\mathrm V$ retarder collects $I_0$; here $I_0$ is the incident <electric current> with fixed geometrical acceptance and no background. The mean outgoing <kinetic energy> is $1.925\,\mathrm{eV}$, independently checked by $5.6-0.75(4.9)$. These are chosen inputs and calculated fractions, not historical measurements.

More generally, let $f(E;V)$ be the normalized outgoing <probability density function> at a fixed accelerating <electric potential difference> $V$, for the normally incident idealization. A retarding scan gives
$$
\frac{I(V,V_r)}{I_0}=\int_{eV_r}^{\infty}f(E;V)\,dE,\qquad
f(eV_r;V)=-\frac{1}{eI_0}\frac{\partial I}{\partial V_r}.
$$
The second equation follows by differentiating the lower integration limit, with <Dirac delta functions> understood for sharp beams. It explains why an integrated collector readout can reveal an <energy> distribution. With angles present it probes the distribution of normal <kinetic energy>; finite instrumental resolution smooths it, and numerical <differentiation> amplifies noise. Keep the incoming beam fixed during this scan. A scan of $V$ changes $f$ itself and cannot be differentiated by the same inversion formula.

= Franck-Hertz repeated-loss collection model
{c}
{parent=Franck-Hertz energy-loss model}
{title2=$E_n=E_0+e(V-V_c)-n\Delta$}

Define a signed offset $V_c$ so that the accelerating region adds <kinetic energy> $e(V-V_c)$. This convention concerns this gap; collector contact fields belong in the effective $V_r$. An <electron> emitted with initial <kinetic energy> $E_0$ and undergoing $n$ identical excitation losses reaches the grid with
$$
E_n=E_0+e(V-V_c)-n\Delta.
$$
Each loss excites a fresh <chemical atom> initially in its <ground state>. These are repeated payments of the same excitation cost. The index $n$ counts events along an <electron> trajectory, not the ordinal number of an atomic <excited state>. The outgoing <energy> also does not locate the last collision: after an early collision an <electron> can regain enough <kinetic energy> to cross the barrier.

At fixed $V$, let $g(E_0)$ be the normalized initial-<energy> <probability density function> within the ensemble of <electrons> reaching the grid, and $w_n(E_0,V)$ the <conditional probabilities> for $n$ losses within that ensemble. Then the ideal collection law is
$$
\frac{I(V,V_r)}{I_0}=\int_0^\infty g(E_0)\sum_{n=0}^\infty w_n(E_0,V)H\bigl(E_0+e(V-V_c)-n\Delta-eV_r\bigr)\,dE_0.
$$
Take $\sum_n w_n=1$ and exclude paths with insufficient <kinetic energy> for a loss at its actual collision position. The weights contain collision positions and rates, <mean free paths> and angular acceptance. This equation is a bookkeeping model, not a derivation of those weights. Multiplying all incident particles by one constant transmission is an additional approximation; real transmission and $I_0$ can vary with $V$.

One analytical extreme assumes $E_0=0$, one-dimensional motion and an immediate loss whenever the <kinetic energy> reaches $\Delta$. With $d=\Delta/e$, $x=V-V_c\geq0$ and $0<V_r<d$, the number of losses and remaining <kinetic energy> are
$$
n=\left\lfloor\frac{x}{d}\right\rfloor,\qquad \frac{E_n}{e}=r=x-d\left\lfloor\frac{x}{d}\right\rfloor,\qquad \frac{I}{I_0}=H(r-V_r).
$$
Here $\lfloor\cdot\rfloor$ is the <floor function>. Collection switches off for an interval of width $V_r$ after each reset, then recovers; the pattern repeats every $d$. For $d=4.9\,\mathrm V$, $V_c=1.2\,\mathrm V$ and $V_r=1.0\,\mathrm V$, an applied $V=11.4\,\mathrm V$ gives $n=2$, $r=0.4\,\mathrm V$ and rejection, whereas $V=12.2\,\mathrm V$ gives $n=2$, $r=1.2\,\mathrm V$ and collection. With $V_r=0$ the dips disappear in this idealization. With no collisions, there is only a single collection onset. Both limits expose the separate roles of loss and filtering.

To draw a smooth illustrative curve, extend this periodic rule mathematically to all $x$ and convolve it with a chosen <normal distribution> of voltage jitter, of <standard deviation> $\sigma$:
$$
C_\sigma(x)=\sum_{k\in\mathbb Z}\left[\Phi\!\left(\frac{x-kd-V_r}{\sigma}\right)-\Phi\!\left(\frac{x-(k+1)d}{\sigma}\right)\right].
$$
Here $\Phi$ is the <standard normal cumulative distribution function>. Each summand is the smoothed indicator of the accepted interval $kd+V_r<x<(k+1)d$. The result satisfies $0\leq C_\sigma\leq1$ and has period $d$. For the figure choose $\sigma=0.18\,\mathrm V$ and plot only well beyond the startup region. This is a chosen phenomenological curve, not a <Boltzmann equation> transport prediction or a fit to measured <electric current>. Its symmetric troughs center on $V_c+nd+V_r/2$, rather than the reset voltages $V_c+nd$. Finite excitation rates, several loss channels, <space charge>, initial angular and <energy> spreads, and real electrode geometry can change the trough shape and phase; a single convolution does not solve these effects.

\Image[franck-hertz-repeated-loss-spacing-fit.png]
{title=Illustrative repeated-loss collection and an independent synthetic spacing fit}
{description=Left: the chosen Gaussian-smoothed threshold model, with $d=4.9\,\mathrm V$, $V_c=1.2\,\mathrm V$, $V_r=1.0\,\mathrm V$ and $\sigma=0.18\,\mathrm V$. Right: separately chosen synthetic trough positions, their <ordinary least squares> fit and residuals. The points are not extracted from the left curve or a historical trace.}
{source=franck-hertz-repeated-loss-spacing-fit.py}

= Franck-Hertz spacing fit and contact offset
{c}
{parent=Franck-Hertz energy-loss model}
{title2=$V_n=b+nd,\quad\Delta=ed$}

Fit the positions of successive, consistently defined troughs by <linear regression>, $V_n=b+nd+\epsilon_n$. A constant intercept $b$ absorbs the <contact potential difference>, collection phase and initial <energy> effects; $d$ estimates the repeated loss expressed as a voltage. Subtracting successive positions cancels $b$. A line fit uses every position and leaves residuals that can reveal curvature or a misidentified trough. It still assumes that the phase is constant over the fitted range and that all troughs belong to the same loss channel.

For an original numerical example, choose $n=1,2,3,4,5$ and synthetic positions $V_n=(6.62,11.46,16.48,21.30,26.14)\,\mathrm V$. Their successive spacings are $(4.84,5.02,4.82,4.84)\,\mathrm V$. The <ordinary least squares> formulas give
$$
\widehat d=\frac{\sum_n(n-\bar n)(V_n-\bar V)}{\sum_n(n-\bar n)^2}=4.888\,\mathrm V,\qquad
\widehat b=\bar V-\widehat d\,\bar n=1.736\,\mathrm V.
$$
The fitted residuals are $(-0.004,-0.052,0.080,0.012,-0.036)\,\mathrm V$, with zero sum and zero index-weighted sum. Their <residual sum of squares> is $0.01056\,\mathrm V^2$. If independent, equal-<variance>, zero-mean errors describe these positions, the residual variance estimate is $s^2=0.01056/(5-2)=0.00352\,\mathrm V^2$ and the slope <standard error> is $s/\sqrt{10}=0.01876\,\mathrm V$. Thus the inferred model loss is $4.888\,\mathrm{eV}$ with conditional <standard error> $0.01876\,\mathrm{eV}$. These errors summarize only the chosen position scatter; a voltage-scale error or a drifting collection phase is not included. Adjacent spacings share a position error and are therefore correlated even when position errors are independent.

The offset has a sharper <identifiability> limitation. Even if a constant-phase model were exact, an initial beam <kinetic energy> $E_0$ shifts the troughs by $-E_0/e$. For the symmetric illustrative curve, $b=V_c-E_0/e+V_r/2$. The transformation $V_c\mapsto V_c+\alpha$, $E_0\mapsto E_0+e\alpha$ leaves every trough position unchanged. Measuring $b$ alone therefore cannot determine the <contact potential difference>. Relabeling $n\mapsto n+m$ also changes $b$ by $-md$ without changing $d$. An independent offset calibration and a justified collection model are needed before turning a first trough into an excitation threshold. Varying $V_r$ tests this model's predicted half-voltage shift of the trough centers; it is a diagnostic prediction of this chosen curve, not a universal correction for experimental minima.

= First excited state
{parent=Excited state}

The first excited state has the smallest energy strictly above the ground-state energy.

= Eigenstate
{parent=Energy eigenvalue}

An eigenstate of an observable is a <quantum state> represented by an <eigenvector> of that observable.

= Momentum eigenstate
{parent=Eigenstate}

A momentum eigenstate is an eigenstate of the <momentum operator>. In one dimension it is a <plane wave> $e^{ikx}$ with momentum eigenvalue $\hbar k$.
